      <h2>License Exchange Process (After 6 Months)</h2>

      <h3>Step 1: Get Your NIE</h3>
      <p>You'll need your NIE (Foreign ID Number). You get this during initial residency registration. If you have your TIE (residency card), you have your NIE.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Contact or Visit DGT</h3>
      <p>Spain's traffic authority (DGT) handles license exchanges. Book a cita previa (appointment) at DGT offices in your province. Some exchanges require a practical driving test; others just an office visit.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Prepare Documents</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Original home country license</li>
        <li>NIE or passport</li>
        <li>Proof of residency (padrón, utility bill, lease)</li>
        <li>Medical certificate (sometimes required)</li>
        <li>Four passport photos (4x4 cm)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Step 4: Pay Fee and Wait</h3>
      <p>License exchange costs €50-150. Processing takes 2-4 weeks. You'll receive your Spanish license by mail.</p>

      <h2>Buying a Car in Spain</h2>

      <h3>Can I Buy a Car Without a Spanish License?</h3>
      <p>Yes, you can own a car before exchanging your license. You need an NIE to register it, not a Spanish driving license. Once you have your NIE, buy and register with local traffic office (jefatura de tráfico).</p>

      <h3>New vs Used Cars</h3>
      <p><strong>New cars:</strong> Dealership handles most paperwork. Expect 21% VAT, registration fees, and insurance (€100-200/month for basic coverage). New cars: €15,000-40,000+ depending on model.</p>

      <p><strong>Used cars:</strong> Private sales (coches de segunda mano) are common on portals like Milanuncios and Coches.com. No VAT, but you'll negotiate price. Transfer ownership at jefatura de tráfico with seller and new NIE registration.</p>

      <h3>Leasing Instead of Buying</h3>
      <p>Many expats lease short-term (renting cars monthly) through companies like Enterprise or Hertz, then buy once settled. This avoids registration hassles initially and gives you flexibility if you're unsure about staying long-term.</p>

      <h2>ITV: Vehicle Safety Inspection</h2>

      <h3>What Is ITV?</h3>
      <p>ITV (Inspección Técnica de Vehículos) is Spain's mandatory vehicle safety inspection. It's similar to the UK's MOT or US emissions testing. Every car older than 4 years requires ITV every 2 years. New cars first ITV at 4 years old.</p>

      <h3>How to Get ITV</h3>
      <p>Book at an authorized ITV center (search your province at itv.es). Appointment takes 30-60 minutes. Bring:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Vehicle registration (permiso de circulación)</li>
        <li>Proof of insurance</li>
        <li>License plate</li>
        <li>Keys</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>ITV Costs and Passing</h3>
      <p>ITV costs €40-80 depending on vehicle type. Most cars pass routinely if maintained. Common failures: broken lights, worn brakes, faulty emissions, leaking fluids. If you fail, fix the issue and retry within 30 days at no extra charge.</p>

      <p>After passing, you get a sticker (pegatina) showing the next inspection due. Display it on your windscreen.</p>

      <h2>Car Insurance in Spain</h2>

      <h3>Is Insurance Mandatory?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Third-party liability insurance is legally required. Fines for driving uninsured: €300-600. Costs €30-100/month for basic coverage on typical cars.</p>

      <h3>Types of Coverage</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Responsabilidad Civil (Third-Party):</strong> Mandatory, covers damage you cause to others. €30-50/month</li>
        <li><strong>Casco Completo (Full Coverage):</strong> Covers your car damage, theft, vandalism. €60-150/month</li>
        <li><strong>Extra Protections:</strong> Breakdown assistance, legal defense, glass coverage</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Getting Spanish Car Insurance</h3>
      <p>Major Spanish insurers: a leading private insurer, Línea Directa, established private health insurers in Spain. Many have English-language options. You need:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>NIE or passport</li>
        <li>Driving license</li>
        <li>Vehicle registration</li>
        <li>Address in Spain</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Online quotes take 5 minutes. Some expats keep their home country insurance and add Spanish liability; check if your US/UK policy covers Spain.</p>

      <h2>Toll Roads (Autopistas)</h2>

      <h3>Are Spanish Highways Toll Roads?</h3>
      <p>Most interstate highways (autopistas) charge tolls. Some regional roads are free. Toll costs €5-25+ depending on distance and vehicle type. Major toll routes: Barcelona to Madrid, Madrid to Valencia, Barcelona to Perpignan (France).</p>

      <h3>How to Pay Tolls</h3>
      <p><strong>Cash:</strong> Stop at booth, pay attendant. Slow but straightforward.</p>
      <p><strong>Electronic (VIA system):</strong> Get a VIA device (€5-10) for automatic payment. Faster toll lanes. Link to credit card.</p>
      <p><strong>Avoid tolls:</strong> Use free alternative roads (carreteras nacionales and carreteras comarcales). Slower but no cost. Google Maps shows toll vs non-toll routes.</p>

      <h2>Spanish Traffic Rules That Surprise Foreigners</h2>

      <h3>Speed Limits</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Cities: 50 km/h (unless signed lower)</li>
        <li>Rural roads: 90 km/h</li>
        <li>Highways (autopistas): 120 km/h</li>
        <li>Motorways with traffic: varies, watch signs</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Key Rules</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seatbelts:</strong> Mandatory for all passengers, including rear seats. Fine: €200</li>
        <li><strong>Headlights:</strong> Required outside urban areas, even daytime (recent change)</li>
        <li><strong>Handheld phones:</strong> Illegal while driving. €200 fine</li>
        <li><strong>Reflective vests:</strong> Each occupant must have one in car. €200 fine if missing</li>
        <li><strong>Parking:</strong> Blue zones (zona azul) require pay-and-display tickets (€0.50-2/hour)</li>
        <li><strong>Alcohol limit:</strong> 0.5 mg/100ml breath (lower for new drivers: 0.3). Penalties: €300-1,500</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Traffic Cameras and Fines</h3>
      <p>Spain uses extensive speed and red-light cameras. Fines for violations: speeding €100-600, red light €200-500, illegal parking €50-200. Fines sent by post to car owner's address.</p>

      <h2>Parking Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Blue zones (zona azul):</strong> Pay-and-display, buy tickets at tobacco shops or machines</li>
        <li><strong>White zones:</strong> Free parking, time-limited (usually 2 hours)</li>
        <li><strong>Yellow lines:</strong> No parking</li>
        <li><strong>Parking garages:</strong> €1-3/hour in cities, common and secure</li>
        <li><strong>Street parking:</strong> Generally safe but lock car and remove valuables</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>FAQs: Driving in Spain</h2>
      <div class="msv__faq">
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">Can I drive in Spain with my UK/US driving license?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">Yes, temporarily. You can drive with your home country license for 6 months while you have valid residency. After 6 months as a resident, exchange your license for a Spanish one. UK licenses exchange via agreement; US licenses often require a new test.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">What is an International Driving Permit (IDP)?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">An IDP is an official translation of your driving license recognized internationally. Required to rent cars in Spain as a tourist but not required for residents with valid home country licenses (first 6 months). Get one from your home country's automotive authority before traveling.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">What is ITV and how often do I need it?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">ITV (Inspección Técnica de Vehículos) is Spain's vehicle safety inspection, similar to MOT. Mandatory every 2 years for vehicles 4+ years old. Book at authorized centers; inspection takes 30-60 minutes. Cost: €40-80 depending on vehicle type.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">Do I need car insurance in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">Yes, third-party liability insurance is mandatory in Spain. Get from Spanish insurers (a leading private insurer, Línea Directa, etc.) or transfer existing policy. Check for expat-friendly insurers; some require Spanish NIE or residency.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">Are toll roads common in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">Yes. Most highways (autopistas) charge tolls. You can pay in cash or use electronic payment (VIA system). Tolls are €5-25 depending on distance and highway. Use free alternative roads (carreteras) to avoid tolls.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-question">What are the key Spanish traffic rules I should know?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">Seatbelts mandatory, speed limits (50km/h city, 90km/h rural, 120km/h highway), no handheld phones, reflective vests required, headlights on outside cities, red traffic light cameras common, alcohol limit 0.5mg/100ml. Fines for violations are steep (€200-1,500).</div>
        </div>
